{#-
  Gemma-3 / MedGemma tool-use chat template.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The stock google/gemma-3-*-it template (1532 bytes, byte-identical across
  all six Google checkpoints in our zoo) cannot represent an agentic episode at all:

    1. it has no `tools` variable, so vLLM discards the tool schemas at templating and the model is
       never told the tools exist -- measured 0.00 tool calls per rollout;
    2. it raises on any role other than user/assistant, in strict alternation, so a `tool` result
       turn is a hard error even if the schemas did arrive.

  (2) is the deeper one and is why "just add tools to the prompt" is not enough.

  WHAT THIS CHANGES, stated plainly so it can be disclosed rather than hidden:
    * tool schemas are rendered as TEXT into the first user turn, which is Google's documented
      route for Gemma 3 -- its function calling is a PROMPTED capability, not a special token
      format;
    * the model is asked for `[func(arg=value)]`, matching vLLM's `pythonic` parser. This is NOT
      the ```tool_code fence Google's own cookbook shows. It is the format vLLM 0.23.0 can parse,
      and no `gemma3` parser exists in its 43-name registry;
    * `tool` results are presented as user turns, since Gemma has no tool role;
    * the alternation check is dropped, because tool turns violate it by construction.

  A score obtained under this template answers "can a Gemma-3 model be driven through a tool loop
  in a format it was not trained on", NOT "does Gemma 3 do tool use". Any leaderboard row produced
  this way must be marked as served under a substituted template and must never be pooled with rows
  served on their native format.
-#}
{{ bos_token }}
{%- if messages[0]['role'] == 'system' -%}
    {%- if messages[0]['content'] is string -%}
        {%- set system_text = messages[0]['content'] -%}
    {%- else -%}
        {%- set system_text = messages[0]['content'][0]['text'] -%}
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- set loop_messages = messages[1:] -%}
{%- else -%}
    {%- set system_text = "" -%}
    {%- set loop_messages = messages -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- set ns = namespace(prefix=system_text) -%}
{%- if tools -%}
    {#- Build the tool list by string concatenation into a namespace, NOT list.append(): vLLM
        renders chat templates in a SANDBOXED Jinja environment where `.append` raises
        "access to attribute 'append' of 'list' object is unsafe" -> HTTP 400 on every request.
        All four Gemma/MedGemma models failed their tool probe on this line, which would have been
        reported as "the model cannot call tools" had the probe gate not caught it first. -#}
    {%- set tl = namespace(text='') -%}
    {%- for t in tools -%}
        {%- set fn = t['function'] if t.get('function') else t -%}
        {%- set tl.text = tl.text ~ '- ' ~ fn['name'] ~ '(' ~ (fn.get('parameters', {}).get('properties', {}).keys() | join(', ')) ~ ')  ' ~ (fn.get('description', '') | trim) ~ '\n    schema: ' ~ (fn.get('parameters', {}) | tojson) ~ '\n' -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
    {%- set ns.prefix = ns.prefix ~ '\n\nYou have access to these tools. They are the ONLY way to see anything; you cannot answer from memory.\n\n' ~ tl.text ~ '\n\nTo call one, write it ALONE on its own line as a Python-style call inside square brackets, using ONE OF THE NAMES LISTED ABOVE, and then stop. For example, if the list above contains a tool named `X` taking an argument `y`, you would write [X(y="...")]. Never write the words tool_name or argument -- those are placeholders, not tools.\nWrite nothing else on that line. You will be given the result and may then call another. When you are finished, answer normally without brackets.' -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- for message in loop_messages -%}
    {%- if message['role'] == 'assistant' -%}
        {{ '<start_of_turn>model\n' }}
        {%- if message.get('tool_calls') -%}
            {%- for tc in message['tool_calls'] -%}
                {%- set fn = tc['function'] if tc.get('function') else tc -%}
                {#- `arguments` may be a dict or a JSON STRING. There is no `fromjson` filter in
                    vLLM's sandboxed Jinja env (offline test: "No filter named 'fromjson' found"),
                    so handle both shapes without one. This renders the assistant's PAST calls back
                    into context; the model's new output is parsed by --tool-call-parser, not here. -#}
                {%- set args = fn['arguments'] -%}
                {{ '[' ~ fn['name'] ~ '(' }}
                {%- if args is string -%}
                    {{ args }}
                {%- else -%}
                    {%- for k, v in args.items() -%}
                        {{ k ~ '=' ~ (v | tojson) }}{{ ', ' if not loop.last else '' }}
                    {%- endfor -%}
                {%- endif -%}
                {{ ')]' }}
            {%- endfor -%}
        {%- else -%}
            {{ (message['content'] or '') | trim }}
        {%- endif -%}
        {{ '<end_of_turn>\n' }}
    {%- else -%}
        {#- user AND tool both become a user turn: Gemma has no tool role -#}
        {{ '<start_of_turn>user\n' }}
        {{- (ns.prefix ~ '\n\n') if loop.first and ns.prefix else '' -}}
        {%- if message['role'] == 'tool' -%}
            {{ 'Result of the tool call:\n' }}
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if message['content'] is string -%}
            {{ message['content'] | trim }}
        {%- elif message['content'] is iterable -%}
            {%- for item in message['content'] -%}
                {%- if item['type'] == 'image' -%}{{ '<start_of_image>' }}
                {%- elif item['type'] == 'text' -%}{{ item['text'] | trim }}
                {%- endif -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
        {%- endif -%}
        {{ '<end_of_turn>\n' }}
    {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- if add_generation_prompt -%}
    {{ '<start_of_turn>model\n' }}
{%- endif -%}
